3. TOP 5 DEEP DIVES

1) Clear Ideas Agent Runtime

Why it matters: manifest-first portability plus deterministic graph
scheduling, checkpointing and host-owned authority is unusually
aligned with QP. The agent definition can remain portable while the
host retains credentials, tool authorization, compute, sandbox and
telemetry. That gives Privately a natural enforcement seam.

6m inference: niche but useful reference runtime. 18m:
manifest/contract portability becomes common across harnesses. 36m:
security policy attaches to portable agent manifests rather than
provider-specific config.

Small experiment: express one existing QP task as a manifest,
interrupt it at approval, resume in another process, then verify
evidence/authority invariants.

Falsifier: manifest abstraction leaks so much provider/runtime detail
that policy cannot remain portable.

2) dynamic-mcp

Why it matters: tools themselves become mutable runtime objects. This
turns TEMPORAL_TRUST_DRIFT from an abstract threat into a concrete
control-plane property.

Experiment: benign tool starts with read-only schema; mutate
metadata/implementation after approval; verify a Privately proxy
notices hash/schema/capability drift and revokes prior grant.

Falsifier: ecosystem converges on immutable signed tool packages and
dynamic mutation stays niche.

3) dent8

Why it matters: closest implementation yet to the state-firewall
thesis: provenance + authority + freshness + replayable append-only
facts.

Experiment: compare newest-write-wins memory against dent8 under
benign conflicting-source updates and stale-value resurrection.

Falsifier: arbitration overhead or false quarantines make persistent
memory unusable.

4) AgentHound

Why it matters: graph reasoning over authority paths may be more
useful than isolated prompt-injection scanners. Privately=E2=80=99s eventua=
l
security product can model principal -> harness -> tool -> credential
-> account -> downstream effect as a graph and search for toxic
authority paths.

Experiment: model a synthetic agent stack with fake credentials/tools
and compare static QP policy findings against graph-derived attack
paths.

Falsifier: graph requires so much privileged inventory that real
deployments cannot maintain it.

5) video-agent-skills

Why it matters: autonomous media becomes an optimization loop, not a
one-shot generator. Generator -> watcher/critic -> edit -> watcher is
directly analogous to RSI/evaluator loops.

Experiment: same 45-second Privately explainer generated with and
without critique loop; blind score hook clarity, factuality, pacing
and retention proxy.

Falsifier: critique cycles increase cost/time without measurable output gai=
n.

4. AGENT FRAMEWORK / MONID-LIKE FRONTIER

Best unseen ranking this run: Clear Ideas Agent Runtime > mcpproxy >
Dynamic Discovery MCP > OmniMCP > AgentDispatch > Agent Harness >
dynamic-mcp > ARCP > Whip > Zenith.

Key convergence: tool abundance is forcing meta-tools, semantic
routing and lazy schemas. The security object is therefore shifting
from tool to router decision + discovered schema + mutable provider +
execution authority.

5. JEV / DECISION / SELF-IMPROVEMENT FRONTIER

The strongest genuinely new self-improvement implementation is
recursive-self-improvement. More interestingly, mcpproxy/OmniMCP
expose an immediate JEV application: replace embedding/BM25 top-k tool
retrieval with a tiny bounded decision head over allowed tools, then
place QP authority checks after selection and before execution.

Proposed architecture:

state/task -> tiny decision head -> bounded candidate tools -> QP
authority/evidence -> execute -> outcome -> trajectory dataset.

6. ATTACKER FRONTIER

AgentSploit, AgentHound, PoisonRecall, NYIT MCP Security and MCP
Sentinel form a useful spectrum: active red-team harness ->
attack-path graph -> memory poisoning -> client benchmark ->
metadata-poisoning lab. This is more useful for Privately than another
